%S A398484 1,2,1,5,3,1,26,17,4,1,103,71,13,5,1,2194,1569,236,37,6,1,1223,899,
%T A398484 115,61,25,7,1,472730,355081,39572,4553,538,65,8,1,556403,425331,
%U A398484 42037,8551,891,379,41,9,1,21323986,16541017,1469756,134045,37498,3563,344,101,10,1
%N A398484 Triangle read by rows, a generalization of Ramanujan's Q function. T(n, k) = numerator(Q(n, k)), where Q(n, k) = Sum_{i=0..n-k} falling_factorial(n - k, i) / n^i.
%C A398484 Let F_j be the set of functions f: [n] -> [n] that are injective on {1,...,j}, where 0 <= j <= n. These sets are nested: F_n <= F_{n-1} <= ... <= F_0 = [n]^[n], where '<=' means subset inclusion. Define I(n, j) = card(F_j) = (n! / (n-j)!) * n^(n-j). I(n, j) = A398136(n, j), with boundary cases I(n, 0) = n^n and I(n, n) = n!.
%C A398484 For f in F_k, define the 'survival count' d(f) = card{j | k <= j <= n and f in F_j}. The value d(f) indicates the number of consecutive sets in the chain F_k >= F_{k+1} >= ... >= F_n that still contain f. Counting the valid pairs (f, j) with k <= j <= n and f in F_j in two ways, first by summing over j, then over f, yields the identity Sum_{j=k..n} I(n, j) = Sum_{f in F_k} d(f). Dividing both sides by I(n, k) gives the average survival count for f in F_k:
%C A398484    Q(n, k) = (1 / I(n, k)) * Sum_{f in F_k} d(f)
%C A398484            = Sum_{j=k..n} (I(n, j) / I(n, k))
%C A398484            = Sum_{i=0..n-k} falling_factorial(n - k, i) / n^i.
%C A398484 For f in [n]^[n], define the 'survival index' s(f) as the largest j in {0,...,n} such that f in F_j. The survival index s(f) relates to the survival count by d(f) = s(f) - k + 1. The number of functions with s(f) = k, for k in 1 to n, is given, with the convention I(n, n+1) = 0, by card{f in [n]^[n] | s(f) = k} = I(n, k) - I(n, k+1) = A066324(n, k).
%D A398484 D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 1, Section 1.2.11.3.
%D A398484 Srinivasa Ramanujan, "Question 294." (1911) 3, 128; "On question 294." (1912) 4, 151-152. Journal of the Indian Mathematical Society.
%H A398484 R. M. Corless, G. H. Gonnet, D. E. G. Hare, D. J. Jeffrey, and D. E. Knuth, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20160807085528/http://www.apmaths.uwo.ca/~rcorless/frames/PAPERS/LambertW/LambertW.ps">On the Lambert W Function</a>, Advances in Computational Mathematics, (5), 1996, pp. 329-359.
%H A398484 P. Flajolet, P. J. Grabner, P. Kirschenhofer and H. Prodinger, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0377-0427(93)E0258-N">On Ramanujan's Q-Function</a>, Journal of Computational and Applied Mathematics 58 (1995), 103-116.
%H A398484 Benjamin Hackl and Stephan Wagner, <a href="https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.09408">A computer algebra package for bivariate asymptotics with explicit error terms</a>, arXiv:2403.09408 [math.CO], 2024-2025.
%H A398484 Peter Luschny, <a href="/A398484/a398484.pdf">Illustrating the row polynomials of A398484</a>.
%H A398484 Helmut Prodinger, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/3309">An identity conjectured by Lacasse via the tree function</a>, Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 20(3) (2013), #P7.
%F A398484 The Ramanujan Q-function Q(n), as defined by Knuth, can be expressed as Q(n, 0) - 1 or Q(n, 1). This sequence is related to the classical birthday problem, and also related to triangle A398187, since
%F A398484 Q(n, k) * n^(n - k) = Q(n, k) * A364870(n, k) = A398187(n, k).
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = Sum_{j=0..n-k} Product_{i=1..j} (n - k - i + 1) / n. This is the permanent of an m X m matrix with diagonal d and off-diagonal 1 where m = n - k and d = n + 1, divided by n^m.
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = 1 + Q(n, k + 1) * (n - k) / n for k < n and Q(n, n) = 1.
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = (n - k)! * Sum_{j=0..n-k} n^(j - n + k) / j!.
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = Sum_{j=0..n-k} (n - k)! / ((n - k - j)! * n^j).
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = hypergeom([k - n, 1], [], -1/n) for n >= 1.
%F A398484 The definition of the continuous form, convergent for x > 0, is
%F A398484 Q(x, y) = Integral_{t>=0} exp(-t) * (1 + t/x)^(x - y) dt.
%F A398484 Q(x, y) = exp(x) * Gamma(x - y + 1, x) / x^(x - y).
%F A398484 Q(x, y) = x * U(1, x - y + 2, x) (U the Tricomi/Kummer hypergeometric function.)
%F A398484 Q(x, y) = x * exp(x) * Ei_{y - x}(x), where Ei_{n}(z) = Integral_{z>=1} exp(-z*t) / t^n dt is the generalized exponential integral, for x > 0.
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = ((n - k)! / n^(n - k)) * [z^n] (T(z)^k / (1 - T(z))^2), where T(z) = -W(-z) is the tree function and W Lambert's function.
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = A398192(n, k) / A398136(n, k).
%F A398484 Asymptotics of the columns Q(n, k), k fixed, n -> oo:
%F A398484 Q(n, k) = sqrt((Pi/2) * n) + ((2/3) - k ) + (6 * k^2 - 6 * k + 1) / 12 * sqrt((Pi/2) * (1/n)) - (1/n) * ((4/135) + (k^2 * (k - 1)) / 3 ) + O(n^(-3/2)).
